  ('745304a3-5032-4eab-b574-ef0a8e5db776','Thunderstruck in Tampa runs a genuinely broad slate of programming as a USA Archery-affiliated hybrid facility: a Junior Olympic Archery Development (JOAD) track, a separate Adult Archery Program, and both 3D archery and field archery on top of that. That combination means the facility can serve a kid just starting Olympic-style recurve alongside an adult bowhunter working 3D targets, all under the same roof. Tampa''s size and year-round warm climate support that kind of full-service model well, giving archers of nearly any interest a program to plug into. No lane counts or additional amenities are listed publicly, so anyone interested in a specific program should contact Thunderstruck directly for current schedules and enrollment.'),
  ('887005ed-ac74-4521-9c0a-39d696697d27','Mesa''s archery scene runs partly through Timber Mesa Archers, a USA Archery affiliated club that treats coaching as its backbone rather than an afterthought. Instruction is available for newcomers working on form, and the club runs Junior Olympic Archery Development for young shooters building toward competition, alongside an Adult Archery Program for grown-ups picking up a bow for the first time or returning to one after years away. A 3D Archery program rounds out the offering for members who want to test their aim on lifelike animal targets instead of paper. It''s a hybrid setup built for archers who want structure and progression, not just a place to shoot.'),
