  ('be25579c-7656-480a-9d7e-355ef9a16963','Whistling Arrow Archery in Montpelier, Vermont appears in the IBO pro shop directory, tying it to the International Bowhunting Organization''s network of retailers and 3D bowhunting circuit rather than operating as an unaffiliated local shop. Vermont''s archery scene skews heavily toward bowhunters given the state''s hunting culture, and an IBO-listed shop like this one likely serves that crowd first, whether through gear, tuning, or pointing customers toward area 3D shoots. No lane counts, lesson programs, or additional amenities are listed beyond the IBO connection, so it reads as a modest operation on paper. Confirming current hours, inventory, and services is best done by contacting the shop directly rather than relying on the directory listing alone.'),
  ('1a089148-beef-424c-917d-1bb18b4a5a43','White County Archery Club, based in Norris City, Illinois, is an ASA-affiliated volunteer club serving the southeastern corner of the state near the Indiana and Kentucky borders. That ASA tie places it within the 3D archery and bowhunting competition circuit common to rural clubs in this region, where scored animal-target rounds tend to draw more participation than pure target shooting. Scott Fiala is listed as the contact for the club, the right person to reach for questions about membership, shoot schedules, or current course conditions. As with most volunteer-run operations, there''s no posted retail presence or public hours, so anyone interested should plan on reaching out directly rather than dropping by unannounced.'),
  ('ddbfe3a4-4549-4cb6-b43d-e47fe780b356','White River Bowhunters, Inc. operates out of Mitchell, Indiana as an ASA-affiliated club, placing it in the same 3D archery and scored bowhunting circuit that many southern Indiana clubs compete in. The Bowhunters name and ASA affiliation together suggest a membership oriented around realistic 3D targets and hunting-season tune-ups rather than Olympic-style target rounds. Mike Baldwin is listed as the contact, a good first call for questions about joining, shoot dates, or the state of the course before visiting. Like most volunteer clubs of this size, there''s no indication of posted hours, a retail shop, or formal lesson programs, so getting current details means reaching out directly rather than searching online.'),
  ('148d7cbc-2b2e-4ad4-bb58-0cb2119c503f','In the Ozarks town of Kirbyville, Missouri, White River Bowmen runs as an ASA-affiliated volunteer club, part of the same scored 3D archery circuit that draws bowhunters across the region each season. The club lists Cassaundra or Richie Brown as contacts, giving prospective members or visitors two names to reach out to for questions about shoot schedules, membership, or course access. Southern Missouri''s Ozark terrain is well suited to 3D bowhunting courses, and ASA-affiliated clubs like this one typically structure much of their year around competitive shoots rather than casual open access. No pro shop, lessons, or public hours are listed, so contacting the club ahead of a visit is the practical first step.'),
  ('c18e7336-a418-492c-8093-81e7cd8983eb','Wilcox JOAD Archery Club in Newport News, Virginia is a USA Archery affiliated club built around the Junior Olympic Archery Development pathway, giving young archers in the Hampton Roads area a structured route from beginner instruction toward competitive shooting. The club also runs an Adult Archery Program and offers lessons, so it''s not exclusively focused on youth despite the JOAD name leading the description. That combination of a nationally recognized junior pipeline plus adult programming makes it a fairly complete option for families or individuals new to the sport in coastal Virginia. Specific details on lanes, facilities, or scheduling aren''t listed, so reaching out to the club directly is the best way to get started.'),
  ('a25f64e6-bdc6-4ef5-bc21-74670428e6ee','Wild Arrow at Plattin Peaks operates as an ASA-affiliated volunteer club in Festus, Missouri, south of St. Louis, tying it into the same 3D archery and bowhunting competition circuit found across much of the Midwest. The Peaks reference in its name hints at hilly terrain suited to a 3D course layout, which fits with its ASA affiliation and the scored animal-target format that style of shooting typically involves. Steve Wolk is listed as the contact, the right person to reach for questions about membership, shoot dates, or course conditions before visiting. As with most clubs of this type, there''s no indication of a retail shop or public hours, so a direct inquiry is the way to get current information.'),
  ('c6ef1afe-d9f5-4847-a8f4-177414425013','Wildwood Bowmen, located in Delta, Ohio in the northwest corner of the state near Toledo, is an ASA-affiliated volunteer club that ties into the broader 3D archery and bowhunting circuit those clubs typically compete on. Randy Eyre is listed as the club''s contact, a useful first call for anyone wanting details on membership, shoot dates, or course access before making a visit. Northwest Ohio''s farmland and woodlots support a solid bowhunting population, and a club named Bowmen with an ASA tie likely leans toward that hunting-focused crowd rather than pure target archery. No pro shop, lessons, or public hours are listed, so a direct inquiry to Eyre is the way to get current information.'),
  ('01ef3706-26e1-40c8-bb83-2aa4e669e063','Winchester Archery, LLC in Cape Girardeau, Missouri is an ASA-affiliated volunteer club, and the surname of its listed contact, Wade Winchester, appears to be where the club''s name comes from rather than any tie to a firearms brand. Southeast Missouri along the Mississippi River has a solid bowhunting tradition, and the ASA affiliation points to a membership that competes in scored 3D rounds through the season. Wade Winchester is the person to reach for questions about joining, shoot dates, or current course conditions, since the club doesn''t appear to maintain a public storefront or posted hours. As with most clubs structured this way, a direct call or message is the reliable way to get current details.'),
  ('1a40fe28-9b70-4cd3-ad2a-d2c70f4dd6ba','Wingfoot Bowhunters Club in Suffield, Ohio carries a club-style name despite being tagged as a commercial facility, and it appears in the IBO pro shop directory while running both a 3D course and a separate field archery course on site. That combination gives shooters access to the scored animal-target format 3D archery is built around, plus the longer-distance field rounds that suit a different kind of practice. Northeast Ohio has a strong bowhunting following, and a facility with an IBO tie plus two distinct course types likely draws a serious crowd of hunters tuning up before season alongside dedicated 3D and field competitors. Specific hours and pricing aren''t listed, so confirming details ahead of a visit is worthwhile.'),
  ('e2dffbb9-594d-4b55-ab8e-e71fa06c75f3','Winks Sporting Goods in Princess Anne, Maryland is a commercial operation on Maryland''s Eastern Shore that combines a genuine pro shop with a 3D archery course and lessons, plus a listing in the IBO pro shop directory tying it to the bowhunting-focused 3D circuit. That''s a fairly complete package for the area, letting archers buy or service gear, shoot a scored 3D course, and get instruction all through the same business rather than piecing services together from multiple places. The Eastern Shore''s rural, hunting-heavy character fits well with an IBO-affiliated shop like this one, and the lesson offering suggests it welcomes newcomers alongside its more experienced bowhunting customers. Hours and current inventory are best confirmed directly with the shop.'),
  ('404dcae4-9e2f-4992-9f01-2d9939b0cb68','Winterhawk Archery Club, based in Safford, Arizona in the state''s southeastern corner, was ASA-affiliated, though available records list that affiliation as expired in 2025. That doesn''t necessarily mean the club has closed, but its formal tie to the ASA 3D competition circuit may no longer be current, so anyone relying on that affiliation for event eligibility should double-check directly with the club. Southeastern Arizona''s desert and mountain terrain supports a fair amount of bowhunting, and a club here likely still serves local members regardless of its ASA standing. Confirming current status, membership, and course access is best done by reaching out to the club itself before making plans.'),
  ('1bfa4df1-e651-4055-b6b2-4686afef533d','Wisconsin Archery Learning Center in Janesville is a USA Archery affiliated facility running the Junior Olympic Archery Development program alongside both 3D and field archery, plus lessons and an on-site pro shop. That range of programming, from a structured youth pathway to two distinct course formats, suggests a facility built to serve archers at very different stages, from beginners taking a first lesson to competitive JOAD athletes and 3D or field shooters. Southern Wisconsin has a solid archery following, and a center offering this much under one name is well positioned as a regional hub rather than a niche stop. Specific hours and pricing aren''t listed, so a direct inquiry is worthwhile before visiting.'),
  ('18c262cd-8746-4acd-9509-457907e9b017','Wo-Pe-Na Archers in Clifton, New Jersey is a USA Archery affiliated club running an Adult Archery Program across 14 lanes, a specific detail that sets it apart from many listings that don''t disclose facility size. Fourteen lanes is a meaningful indoor capacity, suggesting the club can handle a good number of shooters at once rather than operating as a small backyard-style range. The adult-focused programming points to a membership geared toward grown archers picking up the sport or returning to it, rather than a youth-first pipeline like JOAD. Northern New Jersey has a dense population within reach of Clifton, and a 14-lane facility here likely draws from a wide area. Contacting the club directly is the way to confirm scheduling and cost.')
) AS v(id, description)
WHERE r.id = v.id::uuid AND r.is_claimed = false;
